{"step_index":1856,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:20:31Z","input_tokens":2511,"cache_read_tokens":73536,"output_tokens":2669,"thinking":"Initial assessment: The component `CaSlipModal.js` isn't directly involved in the problematic `taxData`; rather, it uses the `invoice` and `audit` objects. The new feature requires a tabbed interface for the invoice review view.\n\n\nThe existing invoice review interface needs to remain as-is. The new feature requires a tax planning view accessible via a tab.\n\nThis new tab must present a customizable tax engine allowing the shopkeeper to select their tax regime and filing scheme, including options for a regular business mode with manual expense inputs or a presumptive taxation mode with adjustable digital and cash sales percentages. This tab also requires a panel for custom deductions and exemptions.\n\n\nThe tax planning view necessitates distinct sections based on the selected tax regime (old or new). For the old regime, input fields are required for deductions under Sec 80C and 80D, plus standard deductions. The new regime will automatically apply the standard deduction and show eligibility for the Sec 87A rebate.\n\nA dynamic tax computation engine will be built to calculate taxes, detailing slab-wise calculations, surcharges, and total estimated tax. It should also show the effective tax rate. An ITC audit card is required to assess eligible Input Tax Credit (ITC) from invoice data, determining eligibility for business resale and indicating ineligible credits based on Section 17(5). It will also estimate net GST outflow.\n\n\nThe enhanced tax computation engine needs a flexible input structure. The existing `calculateTaxMetrics` function will be kept and augmented, potentially with a `calculateComprehensiveTax` variant. Input parameters will include tax regime, filing scheme, revenue figures, and detailed expense categories. The calculation will compute profit based on either the standard ITR-3 business model or a presumptive income scheme, considering digital/cash sales and applicable deductions.\n\n\nThe tax computation will involve two modes: standard business and presumptive taxation, each requiring specific calculations. Total expenses will be derived from the cost of goods sold and operating expenses, impacting the calculation of gross profit and net taxable profit. Audited bills allow for auto-filling or manual input of subtotal values. Presumptive mode calculations will determine digital and cash sales, then compute profit and net taxable profit. Deductions and rebates are calculated differently under the old and new regimes. Under the old regime, deductions like 80C and 80D are considered with their caps to find the net taxable income; rebates under Section 87A are applied if applicable.\n\n\nStandard deduction of ₹75,000 will be automatically applied. Tax calculation now incorporates specific income slabs with corresponding rates based on the budget for the fiscal year 2025-26. Section 87A rebate will be applied based on the net taxable income under the new regime, potentially resulting in zero tax liability. A 4% health and education cess is added to the net tax. Effective tax rate will be calculated based on gross revenue.\n\nA GST Input Tax Credit (ITC) engine is needed to verify invoice taxes. Supplier GSTIN validation is essential to avoid potential credit disallowance under Section 16(2).  A toggle will determine eligibility based on purchase type (resale/capital use or not) which affects ITC eligibility under Section 17(5).\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"read_file","args":{"EndLine":"60","Path":"\"/Users/pranit405/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/audit-it/index.html\"","StartLine":"1"}}]}
